  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How words end",
     "texto": [
      "A Vietnamese syllable can only end in a few consonants. Sometimes the vowel changes the consonant, and sometimes the consonant changes the vowel.",
      "**-m**: «m» with the lips closed. **-n**: «n».",
      "**-p, -t, -c**: they are cut off without releasing the air (the mouth stays in position, without the release of the «p», the «t» or the «k»).",
      "**-c** after **ô, o, u**: the lips also close, as in **-p** (học, độc, cọc).",
      "**-ng**: like the «ng» in «sing», without the «g» sound. After **ô, o, u** the lips also close, as in **-m** (không, đồng, chung).",
      "**-ch**: in the north, like a cut-off «k»; in the south, like **-t**, and the vowel changes a little.",
      "**-nh**: in the north, like **-ng** (anh ≈ «ahng»); in the south, like **-n**, and the vowel changes a little.",
      "**Useful rule:** words that end in **-c, -p, -t, -ch** can only take the **sắc** or **nặng** tone. Never the other four!"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
